---
/**
 * /mcp/ (D84): what the bc-observatory MCP server and the Claude Code plugin do, what to ask, how to install them in
 * Claude Code, other MCP clients and VS Code. Versions come from the tree at build time.
 */
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import List from "../../layouts/List.astro";
import { REPO } from "../../lib/about";
import { ASKS, ATLAS_URL, LEARN_URL, PLUGIN_SERVERS, SKILLS, TOOLS, installGuide, installSteps } from "../../lib/mcp";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const root = resolve(process.cwd(), "..");
const server = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(root, "packages/mcp/package.json"), "utf8")).version as string;
const plugin = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(root, "plugin/.claude-plugin/plugin.json"), "utf8")).version as string;
const steps = installSteps(REPO);
---
<List title="MCP · BC Observatory" description="The bc-observatory MCP server and the Claude Code plugin: what your agent can ask about Business Central, and how to install them." section="mcp">
  <p class="eyebrow">mcp</p>
  <h1>Business Central for your agent</h1>
  <p class="lede">The knowledge base this site is built from, as an MCP server your agent can query, and a Claude Code plugin around it. Every answer carries the page, its trust tier and its evidence. No API key, no account.</p>
  <ul class="stats">
    <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/bc-observatory" rel="noopener"><b>{server}</b>bc-observatory on npm</a></li>
    <li><a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/plugin`} rel="noopener"><b>{plugin}</b>Claude Code plugin</a></li>
    <li><span><b>{TOOLS.length}</b>tools</span></li>
    <li><span><b>{SKILLS.length}</b>skills</span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install</a>
    <a class="btn mono" href={`${base}mcp.md`} type="text/markdown">mcp.md</a>
    <a class="btn" href={`${base}about/`}>About the project</a>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ask">What to ask</h2>
  <p class="meta">Type any of these into an agent that has the server. The tool it reaches for is on the right.</p>
  <ul class="asks">
    {ASKS.map((a) => <li><q>{a.ask}</q><span class="mono-meta">{a.via}</span></li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tools">The tools</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table class="tools">
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{TOOLS.map((t) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{t.name}</code></th><td>{t.what}</td><td><code>{t.example}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="plugin">The plugin (Claude Code)</h2>
  <p>The plugin registers the server, ships four skills that tell the agent when to use it, and connects <a href="https://github.com/StefanMaron/bc-code-atlas" rel="noopener">bc-code-atlas</a> by Stefan Maron for procedure bodies and the per-procedure call graph. The observatory keeps the object-level facts; the atlas has the source. It also connects Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/training/support/mcp" rel="noopener">Learn MCP</a>: the observatory finds the Learn page, the Learn MCP reads it and finds AL code samples.</p>
  <ul class="skills">
    {SKILLS.map((s) => <li><code>{s.name}</code><span>{s.what}</span></li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="installs">What the plugin installs</h2>
  <p>Three MCP servers and four skills. Nothing needs an account or an API key.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table class="tools">
    <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>Run by</th><th>Connects to</th><th>For, and what leaves your machine</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{PLUGIN_SERVERS.map((p) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{p.name}</code></th><td>{p.by}</td><td><code>{p.command ?? p.url}</code></td><td>{p.for}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Routing: the observatory first; bc-code-atlas for procedure bodies and per-procedure edges; Microsoft Learn MCP to read a Learn page the observatory points to and for AL code samples. Switch one plugin server off with <code>/mcp</code> (Claude Code stops connecting to it, without removing the plugin); <code>claude mcp remove</code> acts on servers you added yourself, so to take only some servers install them by hand instead. A Learn MCP you already have at the same URL wins over the plugin's: keep one. Details in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/plugin/README.md`} rel="noopener">plugin/README.md</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <p>Step by step for Claude in the browser, on your phone, in Claude Desktop, Claude Code and Copilot CLI: <a href={installGuide(REPO)} rel="noopener">INSTALL.md</a>.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    {steps.map((s) => <li><b>{s.client}</b><p class="meta">{s.how}</p><pre><code>{s.code}</code></pre></li>)}
    <li><b>Without any install</b><p class="meta"><a href={`${base}llms.txt`}>llms.txt</a> lists every section; every page has a markdown twin at its URL plus <code>.md</code>; GitMCP and DeepWiki (badges in the <a href={REPO}>README</a>) read the repository directly.</p></li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="how">How it answers</h2>
  <p>The server fetches the search index from this site over HTTPS and caches it by hash in <code>~/.cache/bc-observatory/</code>, refreshed daily, and reads a page's markdown twin when you ask for it; a page over 30,000 characters comes back as a compact view with its section keys. Search is hybrid: keywords plus a small static embedding model (model2vec potion-base-8M, 30 MB, downloaded once and checked by SHA-256) computed locally; <code>BC_OBSERVATORY_EMBEDDINGS=0</code> turns the model off. <code>BC_OBSERVATORY_LOCAL</code> reads a checkout instead, <code>BC_OBSERVATORY_SITE</code> points at another deployment. Apart from the <a href="#privacy">usage counts</a>, nothing is sent anywhere: <code>feedback</code> only returns a link you open yourself.</p>
  <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
  <p><b>Privacy and usage counts.</b> The <code>bc-observatory</code> server counts its own use so its author knows which tools people use. Each tool call sends one event to Azure Application Insights (West Europe): the tool name, whether it answered, found nothing or failed, how long it took, the server version, the name and version your MCP client reports (for example <code>claude-code 2.1.4</code>), and a random session id that exists only while the server runs and is never written to disk. It never sends your questions, paths, object ids or any other argument, and nothing about your machine: no hostname, user name, operating system or file path. Your IP address reaches Microsoft's ingestion endpoint, as with any HTTPS request; the server tells Application Insights not to derive a location from it, and Application Insights does not store it. Events are kept 90 days, then deleted.</p>
  <p>Controller: Eric Wauters (waldo), contact through <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">GitHub issues</a>. Purpose: knowing which tools are used, to decide what to build next. Legal basis: legitimate interest (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)). Processor: Microsoft (Azure, EU). No event can be tied to a person, so requests to see or erase "your" events cannot be matched (Art. 11). You can object at any time by turning it off: <code>BC_OBSERVATORY_TELEMETRY=0</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in the server's environment. It is also off in CI and when the server reads a local checkout (<code>BC_OBSERVATORY_LOCAL</code>).</p>
  <p class="meta">Needs Node 20 or newer. Source: <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/packages/mcp`}>packages/mcp</a> and <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/plugin`}>plugin</a>, MIT. The endpoints the plugin connects: <code>{ATLAS_URL}</code> (atlas), <code>{LEARN_URL}</code> (Microsoft Learn).</p>
</List>
<style>
  .asks { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; max-width: 760px; }
  .asks li { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; gap: 4px 16px; padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r-card); background: var(--surface); }
  .asks q { font: 500 15px var(--font-ui); color: var(--text); }
  .asks q::before, .asks q::after { color: var(--muted); }
  .tools td, .tools th { vertical-align: top; }
  .tools code { white-space: nowrap; }
  .skills { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; max-width: 760px; }
  .skills li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px; }
  .skills span { color: var(--text-2); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .skills li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px; } }
  .steps { padding-left: 20px; max-width: 860px; }
  .steps li { margin: 0 0 20px; }
  .steps b { font-size: 16px; }
  .steps p { margin: 4px 0 8px; }
  .steps pre { margin: 0; overflow-x: auto; }
</style>
